Reflecting on my recent experiences acting on the environment with different strategies has provided valuable insights into what works best for balancing the pole. 

Initially, I had assumed that focusing solely on the pole's angle and angular velocity would be sufficient to keep it balanced. However, as the trials progressed, it became clear that considering the cart's position and velocity was also crucial. My first few strategies performed decently but failed to achieve the highest possible scores.

Upon reviewing my best-performing strategy so far (the one with an average reward of 334.75/500), I noticed that it successfully combined conditions related to both the pole and the cart. The rules regarding the pole's angle and angular velocity were straightforward and seemed to work well in most cases. However, the addition of rules based on the cart's position and velocity significantly improved performance.

A critical observation from this strategy was how effectively it handled scenarios where the pole started to lean heavily in one direction (either positive or negative angle). By including rules that moved the cart in the direction of the lean when the pole's angle was greater than 0 or less than 0, respectively, it managed to correct the pole's position more efficiently. 

For instance, in steps where the pole had a significant positive angle and was increasing (indicating it was falling towards one side), moving the cart to the right helped to balance it out. Similarly, when the pole had a negative angle, moving the cart to the left corrected its position.

Another key factor that contributed to this strategy's success was its handling of the cart's velocity. By adjusting the direction based on whether the cart's velocity was greater than 5 or less than -5, it managed to dampen oscillations effectively and maintain a more stable state for longer periods.

Considering these insights, I am inclined to refine my best-performing strategy further rather than starting from scratch. One potential area of improvement could be adjusting the thresholds used in the rules (e.g., changing "IF abs(Pole angle) > 10" to a different value) to see if finer tuning can lead to better results.

